  ('ut-r-12-a-1-A-responsive-pleading-after-motion-resolved', 'ut', 'sairnlaw_deadline_seed_utah.json', 1, '{"count": {"direction": "forward", "unit": "calendar_days", "value": 14}, "domain": "civil-litigation", "effective_from": "2024-05-01", "effective_to": null, "jurisdiction": "ut", "service_extension": {"add": 7, "applies_when": ["mail"], "note": "URCP 6(c) adds SEVEN days -- not three -- but ONLY where service was made EXCLUSIVELY BY MAIL under Rule 5(b)(3)(C)(i). Seeded 2026-08-27, having been HELD OUT since the Utah gate because this engine could not express exclusivity with one service_method field; the exclusive-vs-combined mechanism built that day is what unblocked it. on_unknown_exclusivity is REFUSE, not assume: a bare service_method of `mail` does not say mail was the ONLY method, and assuming it would report SEVEN DAYS LATE whenever it was not -- far too large an overshoot to guess at, and unlike Florida there is no existing arithmetic to preserve. Rule 5(b)(3)(C) permits mail only where the party \"does not have an electronic filing account or email\", so a Utah party served by mail may well also have been served another way. SUPPLY service_methods as the full list actually used (e.g. [\"mail\"] or [\"mail\",\"email\"]) and the extension is computed; supply only service_method and the engine refuses it visibly and returns the date WITHOUT it, which is EARLY and safe. NOT ON EITHER ANSWER-TO-SUMMONS ROW: 6(c) points at Rule 5(b)(3)(C)(i), and Utah''s summons goes out under Rule 4 -- the same reading that corrected the two federal rows the same day.", "on_unknown_exclusivity": "refuse", "order": "after_base_period", "requires_exclusive": true, "standard": "ut_urcp_6_c", "unit": "calendar_days"}, "trigger_event": "notice_of_court_action_on_rule_12_motion"}'::jsonb),
